  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nAnother pleasant summer day is unfolding, with the upper ridge axis \ncentered from Colorado, northeast to near Winnipeg. The surface high \nis a bit east of the mid-level ridge, centered over WI, with a cold \nfront slowly moving east across the Dakotas. This has a allowed a \npressure gradient to slowly build through the day, with some \nsoutheast gusts up around 25 mph being observed out in western MN. \nThis front will be the source of our next chance for rain, but it \nwill remain well west of us through the day on Monday, with the \nweather Monday expected to be similar to today, just with an uptick \nin dewpoints with the southerly winds. The wave driving this front \nwill be going along the international border, so forcing with the \nfront will be strongest north of us. Late in the day on Monday, we \nshould see some fairly robust convection develop in the Red River \nValley up from Fargo up toward Grand Forks that will then track \nacross northwest MN. There's less confidence in how widespread \nconvection along the front will be south of Fargo, but scattered \nstorms should develop across eastern SoDak late in the day. \nCurrently, it doesn't look like we see any activity into western \nportions of the MPX area until after 10pm Monday evening. At that \npoint, instability will be trending down, as will our threat for \nsevere weather. A smattering of showers will continue along the \nfront through Tuesday morning as it works across southern MN. \nHowever, with the main upper wave working across northwest Ontario, \nother than convergence along the boundary, we don't have much \nforcing for precipitation, which means for most of the MPX area, \nthis front will only offer a few minor showers. Perhaps the biggest \nimpact from the front will be the potential for surface based smoke \nin its wake from wild fires in Washington, Oregon, British Columbia. \nObservations this afternoon show reduced air quality behind the cold \nfront in northeast Montana, though there's a lot of ground for that \nsmoke to cover for it to get here, so this will be something to \nwatch through the day on Monday.\n\nFor the rest of next week, it looks tranquil. The front that comes \nthrough Monday night will spark additional rounds of thunderstorms \nTuesday and Wednesday, but it will be in Iowa when it does so. \nInstead, we get some very pleasant air for Tuesday and Wednesday, \nwith highs near 80 and dewpoints falling back into the 50s, with \nsome 40s even possible into central MN.\n\nBy next weekend, the upper flow will have flattened out. This will \nallow warmer air to spread north, but could also see some frontal \nactivity. There's lots of spread in models with the placement of any \nfronts next weekend. Right now, you can find anything from the \nfronts remaining north of us, with it being hot (90s possible) and \ndry, to it being stormy and unsettled next weekend. Given that \nspread, stuck with NBM at this point, which has highs about 5 \ndegrees above normal, with some low precip chances that peek \nSaturday night.",
